MYSHELL USER MANUAL
===================

NAME
    myshell - a simple command line interpreter

SYNOPSIS
    myshell [batchfile]

DESCRIPTION
    myshell is a simple command line shell that provides a basic command 
    interpreter for Linux systems. It supports both interactive mode and 
    batch mode operation.

    When invoked without arguments, myshell operates in interactive mode,
    displaying a prompt and waiting for user commands. When invoked with
    a batchfile argument, myshell reads and executes commands from the
    specified file.

COMMAND PROMPT
    The command prompt displays the current working directory followed by
    a '>' character:

        /home/user/documents>

INTERNAL COMMANDS
    myshell provides the following built-in commands:

    cd [directory]
        Change the current working directory to <directory>. If no
        directory is specified, displays the current directory path.
        This command also updates the PWD environment variable.

        Examples:
            cd /home/user       Change to /home/user directory
            cd ..               Change to parent directory
            cd                  Display current directory

    clr
        Clear the terminal screen.

        Example:
            clr

    dir [directory]
        List the contents of <directory>. If no directory is specified,
        lists the contents of the current directory. The listing includes
        file names, sizes, and types (FILE or DIR).

        Examples:
            dir                 List current directory
            dir /home/user      List /home/user directory
            dir > output.txt    Redirect listing to file

    environ
        Display all environment variables and their values.

        Example:
            environ

    echo <comment>
        Display the specified text on the screen followed by a newline.
        Multiple spaces and tabs may be reduced to a single space.

        Examples:
            echo Hello World
            echo This is a test > output.txt

    help
        Display this user manual using the 'more' command for pagination.
        The readme file must be present in the current directory.

        Example:
            help

    pause
        Suspend shell operation until the Enter key is pressed.

        Example:
            pause

    quit
        Exit the shell.

        Example:
            quit

EXTERNAL COMMANDS
    Any command that is not an internal command is treated as an external
    program invocation. The shell will search for the program in the
    directories specified by the PATH environment variable, create a child
    process, and execute the program.

    Examples:
        ls -l               List files using system ls command
        gcc hello.c         Compile a C program
        ./myprogram         Execute program in current directory

INPUT/OUTPUT REDIRECTION
    myshell supports redirection of standard input and standard output.

    Input Redirection (<)
        Redirect standard input from a file.

        Syntax:
            command < inputfile

        Example:
            sort < unsorted.txt

    Output Redirection (>)
        Redirect standard output to a file. If the file exists, it will
        be overwritten.

        Syntax:
            command > outputfile

        Example:
            ls -l > filelist.txt
            echo Hello > greeting.txt

    Output Redirection - Append (>>)
        Redirect standard output to a file. If the file exists, output
        will be appended to the end of the file.

        Syntax:
            command >> outputfile

        Example:
            echo Line 1 >> log.txt
            echo Line 2 >> log.txt

    Combined Redirection
        Input and output can be redirected in the same command.

        Example:
            sort < input.txt > output.txt

    Note: The dir and echo internal commands support output redirection.

BACKGROUND EXECUTION
    Commands can be executed in the background by appending an ampersand
    (&) character to the end of the command line. When a command is run
    in the background, the shell immediately returns to the command prompt
    without waiting for the command to complete.

    Syntax:
        command &

    Examples:
        sleep 100 &
        gcc large_project.c &

    The shell will display the process ID (PID) of the background process:
        [Background] PID: 12345

BATCH MODE
    myshell can execute commands from a batch file. To run in batch mode,
    provide the filename as a command line argument when starting the shell:

        myshell mybatch.txt

    In batch mode:
    - Commands are read from the specified file
    - Each command is echoed to the screen before execution
    - The shell exits when the end of the file is reached
    - No command prompt is displayed

    Example batch file content:
        cd /tmp
        echo Starting batch processing
        dir
        echo Batch processing complete

ENVIRONMENT
    The shell sets the following environment variable:

    shell
        Contains the full pathname of the myshell executable.

        Example:
            /home/user/myshell

    The shell also inherits all environment variables from its parent
    process. You can view all environment variables using the 'environ'
    command.

COMMAND LINE SYNTAX
    Command lines consist of a command name followed by optional arguments,
    redirection operators, and control operators. Elements are separated by
    one or more spaces or tabs.

    General syntax:
        command [arg1] [arg2] ... [< input] [> output] [&]

    Notes:
    - Arguments, redirection operators (<, >, >>), and the background
      operator (&) must be separated by whitespace
    - Empty lines are ignored
    - Maximum command line length is 1024 characters
    - Maximum number of arguments is 64

EXAMPLES

    Basic Commands:
        cd /home/user           Change to /home/user directory
        clr                     Clear screen
        dir                     List current directory
        echo Hello World        Display message
        pause                   Pause until Enter pressed
        quit                    Exit shell

    External Programs:
        ls -la                  Run ls command
        ps aux                  Display processes
        ./myprogram arg1        Run local program

    Redirection:
        dir > listing.txt       Save directory listing
        echo Text >> log.txt    Append to log file
        sort < data.txt         Sort file contents
        wc -l < file.txt > count.txt    Count lines

    Background Execution:
        sleep 60 &              Sleep in background
        long_process &          Run process in background

    Batch Mode:
        myshell commands.txt    Execute batch file

NOTES
    - The shell uses the PATH environment variable to locate external
      commands
    - The current working directory is displayed in the prompt
    - Background processes continue to run after the shell exits
    - The help command requires the 'readme' file in the current directory
    - I/O redirection is performed before command execution

LIMITATIONS
    - No command history or editing
    - No tab completion
    - No wildcard expansion (*, ?)
    - No command substitution
    - No piping between commands
    - No job control (fg, bg, jobs)
    - No signal handling customization

ERROR MESSAGES
    The shell provides descriptive error messages for common problems:

    - "command not found": The specified command does not exist
    - "cannot open directory": Directory does not exist or no permission
    - "cannot open file": File redirection failed
    - "fork failed": System unable to create child process
    - "cannot open batch file": Batch file does not exist

